WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:05.770
Film Courage: Okay then I’ll give you my
version of a marginalized voice in a story

00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:07.260
versus a middle class one [first world problems].

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:13.309
A marginalized one is a little girl wants
to go to a birthday party but the friends

00:00:13.309 --> 00:00:17.490
in the neighborhood...the mothers know that
her mom cleans people’s houses for a living.

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:20.340
The [pack] of moms respond with “Oh...honey...she’s
nice but we don’t really know her.

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:22.259
I’m so sorry.”

00:00:22.259 --> 00:00:27.500
The middle class voice is someone is applying
for colleges but it turns out most of her

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:31.519
friends are getting accepted into college
and she’s not sure if her GPA is good enough

00:00:31.519 --> 00:00:32.519
to get into the school her friends will all
attend.

00:00:32.519 --> 00:00:37.510
And [the protagonist] is like “Oh my gosh,
I’m going to lose all of my high school

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:38.510
friends.

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:39.510
What’s going to happen to my social standing
in this group?”

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:41.010
Alan Watt, Author/Screenwriter and [Founder
of L.A. Writers' Lab]: Okay so these are like

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:45.770
seeds of story right?

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:47.129
So tell me what is your question?

00:00:47.129 --> 00:00:52.449
Film Courage: My question is are the stakes
higher for the author of the marginalized

00:00:52.449 --> 00:00:56.949
voice where that story implies non-inclusion
into a group?

00:00:56.949 --> 00:01:03.450
Whereas the middle class story has a protagonist
more worried about losing social footing or

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:04.450
losing touch with friends?

00:01:04.450 --> 00:01:05.960
Are the stakes higher for one versus the other?

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:07.210
Al: I don’t think of stakes...gosh?

00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:13.170
I don’t know how to answer that question.

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:21.700
I don’t think the stakes are about the actual
situation.

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:31.009
The stakes are really about the ability of
the writer to convey a sense of urgency or

00:01:31.009 --> 00:01:32.009
anxiety.

00:01:32.009 --> 00:01:41.560
In other words...we’re not really talking
about dilemma, we’re talking about stakes.

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:46.079
The stakes should always be life and death
in every story.

00:01:46.079 --> 00:01:47.770
They are always life and death.

00:01:47.770 --> 00:01:53.869
If Jan Brady doesn’t get a date with Tad
Hamilton she will absolutely die!

00:01:53.869 --> 00:01:56.369
Stakes are always life and death.

00:01:56.369 --> 00:02:01.229
If they are anything less than life and death,
we don’t care at all.

00:02:01.229 --> 00:02:02.229
We don’t care at all.

00:02:02.229 --> 00:02:11.500
If you want to talk about dilemma, there are
two ingredients to dilemma.

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:17.020
So dilemma is a problem that can’t be solved
without creating another problem.

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:20.760
And when you connect to your dilemma you’re
connecting to the aliveness in your story.

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:29.170
There are two ingredients to a dilemma: A
powerful want, a powerful desire and a false

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:30.170
belief.

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:33.880
A misperception of myself or the world.

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:38.570
And so when Sally loves me, then I’ll be
complete.

00:02:38.570 --> 00:02:41.050
Now that’s the beginning of the story.

00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:43.490
I’ve got to get Sally to love me in order
to be complete.

00:02:43.490 --> 00:02:48.610
Do you see a powerful desire...if Sally doesn’t
love me my life will be unimaginable.

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:54.420
And the false belief is that Sally will complete
me.

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:56.120
So we’ve got a powerful desire.

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:58.780
Remember that story structure is desire, surrender,
transformation.

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:04.030
We’ve got to surrender, I mean I’m going
to have to let go of the meaning I make out

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:08.840
of my goal as long as I believe that Sally’s
love complete’s me, I will forever be in

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:16.920
bondage to Sally or my idea of Sally’s love
because...and the audience or the reader is

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:21.340
going to be disappointed if all that happens
at the end of the story is that Sally loves

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:23.890
me.

00:03:23.890 --> 00:03:28.230
If Jimmy Stewart left Bedford Falls at the
end of IT’S A WONDERFUL LIFE we would be

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:32.010
disappointed because that’s not what the
story is about.

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:35.390
The plot is will Sally love me.

00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:40.890
The them is about completeness.

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:42.900
What does it mean to be complete?

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:47.630
It doesn’t mean that completeness comes
from outside of ourselves, it comes from within.

00:03:47.630 --> 00:03:49.820
Okay so that’s a dilemma.

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:52.370
The dilemma is what is the theme?

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:53.370
It’s completeness.

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:56.510
If you’re doing a story about justice.

00:03:56.510 --> 00:04:00.370
Let’s say I’m in prison...or freedom.

00:04:00.370 --> 00:04:03.840
Let’s say this is a story about what does
it mean to be free.

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:08.920
So if I’m in prison and I want to be free
I might believe at the beginning of the story

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:13.060
that when I escape then I’ll be free.

00:04:13.060 --> 00:04:18.770
That’s dilemma, that’s the dramatic question.

00:04:18.770 --> 00:04:23.950
And then through the story I might actually
succeed in escaping and discover that I’m

00:04:23.950 --> 00:04:25.880
still not free.

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:28.090
That could be the dark night of the soul.

00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:34.870
Let’s say I escape and I’m with a buddy
and we’re shackled and we get away and the

00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:42.760
he trips and twists his ankle and now I’ve
got to decide do I really want to be free.

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:48.310
Now I’ve got to take a blunt instrument
and hurt my buddy, you know what I mean.

00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:50.160
And now am I free?

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:52.420
They are chasing after me.

00:04:52.420 --> 00:05:00.460
So in my attempt to become free, in your protagonist
attempt to get what he or she wants, the movie

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:03.650
increasingly away from their desire.

00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:08.820
So my attempt to be free moves me further
away from freedom leading to the end of Act

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:15.110
2 where I have to let go of the meaning I
made out of my goal...i.e. when I escape prison,

00:05:15.110 --> 00:05:16.110
then I’ll be free.

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:22.870
End of Act 2 I discover that true freedom
comes from within and I might discover at

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:30.530
the end of the story that freedom might involve
going back to prison.

00:05:30.530 --> 00:05:33.430
Taking responsibility for my crime.

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:36.540
So that’s the shift in perception.

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:42.840
So desire (I want to be free), surrender (I
have to let go of the meaning of my false

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:48.220
belief of what freedom means) and transformation
(I have a new understanding of freedom).

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:51.320
Film Courage: I think you had used the movie
THE SOCIAL NETWORK in another interview I

00:05:51.320 --> 00:05:52.320
watched.

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:54.900
Can you use that analogy as well because I
found it interesting.

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:58.350
Al: Oh...yes...that was a long time ago.

00:05:58.350 --> 00:06:04.990
So the dilemma of THE SOCIAL NETWORK is the
tension between integrity and ambition.

00:06:04.990 --> 00:06:12.800
So if I’m really ambitious and I want to
make a billion dollars...it’s the same kind

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:13.800
of thing.

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:16.780
I set out on this quest.

00:06:16.780 --> 00:06:21.920
But did I steal this idea from these two gorgeous
twins?

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:24.940
Film Courage: [Laughs] They were quite handsome.

00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:32.480
Al: And so that’s what THE SOCIAL NETWORK
explores.

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:39.730
Every single scene examines the dilemma of
integrity versus ambition.

00:06:39.730 --> 00:06:41.320
So he is driven by his ambition.

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:42.830
What do I want?

00:06:42.830 --> 00:06:44.770
I want to succeed.

00:06:44.770 --> 00:06:45.770
What do I need?

00:06:45.770 --> 00:06:49.020
I need to do the right thing?

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:54.110
And I think it’s kind of an ambiguous ending
but it leaves us with that question (does

00:06:54.110 --> 00:07:00.810
the end satisfy the means)...or something
like that?

